"Budget Monitoring Report April 2026 - August 2026 - Report","url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200353/budget-monitoring-report-april-2026-august-2026-report.pdf","committee":"Cornwall Inshore Fisheries and Conservation Authority","date":"11/09/2026","key":false},{"title":"Budget Monitoring Report April 2026 - August 2026 - Appendices 1 and 2","url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200354/budget-monitoring-report-april-2026-august-2026-appendices-1.pdf","committee":"Cornwall Inshore Fisheries and Conservation Authority","date":"11/09/2026","key":false},{"title":"Cornwall Pension Fund LPB Governance Update - Report","url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200188/cornwall-pension-fund-lpb-governance-update-report.pdf","committee":"Cornwall Pension Fund Local Pension Board","date":"08/09/2026","key":false},{"title":"Cornwall Pension Fund LPB Governance Update - Appendix 1","url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200189/cornwall-pension-fund-lpb-governance-update-appendix-1.pdf","committee":"Cornwall Pension Fund Local Pension Board","date":"08/09/2026","key":false}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-25","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Sustainable Growth and Place Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Health and Adult Social Care Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-01","committee":"Standing Advisory Council on Religious Education","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-07","committee":"Together for Families Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Cornwall and Isles of Scilly Fire and Rescue Service Local Pension Board","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Corporate Finance and Performance Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Constitution and Governance Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cornwall Pension Fund Local Pension Board","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Community Wellbeing Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cornwall Council","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Pensions Committee","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Cornwall spends \u00a310m a year on temporary housing as register trebles despite record delivery","lead":"Cornwall Council is spending approximately \u00a310 million a year on temporary and emergency accommodation for more than 800 people, even as it achieves record housing delivery. The council's social housing register has grown from around 9,000 households in March 2020 to approximately 25,000 today\u2014an increase of 178 per cent in six and a half years. In 2025/26, the council delivered 824 affordable homes, the highest annual total since before the pandemic. Yet the register continues to grow. The council receives around 500 new applications each month but can house only approximately 120 households, leaving a structural gap between demand and supply that shows no sign of closing.","iso":"2026-09-15","paper_title":"A Fairer, Clearer Housing List for Cornwall - Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200479/200479.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cornwall Council","paper_title":"Capital Programme Update - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200484/200484.pdf","headline":"46% of the forward capital programme is scheduled for 2029/30 and beyond","detail":"The council plans to spend \u00a3841.237m out of its \u00a31,812.850m forward capital programme (46%) in 2029/30 and beyond, deferring nearly half of planned investment to the distant future.","category":"capital","matters":"Concentration of capital spending 5+ years out creates delivery uncertainty and depends on future market conditions and funding stability; limits the council's ability to adapt if circumstances change","figures":"2029/30 & Future 841.237 of Revised Capital Programme 1,812.850 (46%)","quote":"Revised Capital Programme as at Full Council 15 September 2026 1,812.850; 2029/30 & Future 841.237","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cornwall Council","paper_title":"Capital Programme Update - Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200484/200484.pdf","headline":"49% of the forward capital programme will be funded by new prudential borrowing","detail":"Of the \u00a31,812.850m revised capital programme for 2026/27 onwards, \u00a3893.352m (49%) will be funded through new prudential borrowing, making it the single largest funding source. Borrowing intensity rises to 57% in both 2026/27 and 2027/28 before moderating in later years.","category":"borrowing","matters":"High borrowing concentration (nearly half of spending) is material to debt leverage and serviceability; this intensity limits financial flexibility without demonstrated confidence in the council's debt capacity","figures":"Prudential Borrowing 893.352 of Total Funding 1,812.850 (49%); peaks at 177.993 out of 312.397 (57%) in 2027/28","quote":"Prudential Borrowing 159.065 171.029 177.993 194.371 349.959 1,052.418 893.352","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cornwall Council","paper_title":"A Fairer, Clearer Housing List for Cornwall - Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200479/200479.pdf","headline":"Record housing delivery of 824 homes in 2025/26 has not closed the gap between register growth and housing supply","detail":"Despite delivering a record 824 affordable homes in 2025/26, the highest annual total since before the pandemic, the housing register continues to grow. This structural mismatch between demand and even record supply levels suggests the housing pressure and associated costs will persist.","category":"demand","matters":"If record supply rates cannot close the register growth, the underlying demand pressure is structural and may require sustained funding or new approaches to address temporary accommodation costs.","figures":"824 affordable homes delivered in 2025/26 (highest annual total since pre-pandemic); 850+ homes delivered since 2020; register continues to grow despite this level of delivery","quote":"in 2025/26 a total of 824 affordable homes were delivered across Cornwall, the highest annual total since before the pandemic. Even at record delivery, the register has continued to grow, which is why the register itself must work as well as possible.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cornwall Council","paper_title":"A Fairer, Clearer Housing List for Cornwall - Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200479/200479.pdf","headline":"Temporary accommodation for 800+ people costs \u00a310 million annually; housing register grew from 9,000 to 25,000 in six years","detail":"Cornwall's social housing register has grown by 178% in six and a half years. The council is currently placing approximately 120 households per month against 500 new applications, while more than 800 people remain in temporary and emergency accommodation. This temporary housing arrangement costs approximately \u00a310 million annually.","category":"demand","matters":"A recurring \u00a310 million annual cost for temporary accommodation is a significant budget pressure; the register growth outpacing new housing supply suggests this cost burden may continue or grow.","figures":"25,000 households on register (up from 9,000 in March 2020); around 500 new applications per month; approximately 120 households housed per month; more than 800 people in temporary and emergency accommodation; cost of approximately \u00a310 million a year","quote":"Around 25,000 households are on the Cornwall Homechoice register, up from around 9,000 in March 2020, with around 500 new applications received each month while approximately 120 households are housed, and more than 800 people in temporary and emergency accommodation at a cost of approximately \u00a310 million a year.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-11","committee":"Cornwall Inshore Fisheries and Conservation Authority","paper_title":"Budget Monitoring Report April 2026 - August 2026 - Appendices 1 and 2","paper_url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200354/200354.pdf","headline":"The authority's forecast now includes \u00a3202,215 vessel capital funding from the Asset Replacement Reserve that was not in the previous forecast, with a further \u00a381,000 payment planned","detail":"The current full-year forecast adds \u00a3202,215 in 'Contribution from Reserves (Vessel)' that did not appear in the previous forecast. Appendix 2 notes stage payments of \u00a3202,215 with a further payment of approximately \u00a381,000. Combined with other drawdowns, the Asset Replacement Reserve is forecast to fall from \u00a3853,220.58 to \u00a3515,005.58\u2014a reduction of \u00a3338,215 (40%). This capital spending was not included in the previous forecast.","category":"capital","matters":"A council finance director needs to track whether CIFCA's significant reserve depletion (\u00a3338k planned drawdown) and newly-added capital commitments (\u00a3202k not previously forecast) indicate a trend toward reduced financial self-sufficiency that will require increased levy contributions to the authority in future years","figures":"Vessel reserve contribution: \u00a3202,215 (current forecast) vs \u00a30 (previous forecast); further vessel payment c\u00a381,000; Asset Replacement Reserve moves from \u00a3853,220.58 (1 April 2026) to \u00a3515,005.58 (31 March 2027); total drawdown \u00a3338,215","quote":"Vessel stage payment \u00a3202,215 and further payment of c\u00a381,000","material":false}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Cornwall UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-20","since_iso":"2026-08-21","distress":false,"headline":"46% of the forward capital programme is scheduled for 2029/30 and beyond","lead":"Concentration of capital spending 5+ years out creates delivery uncertainty and depends on future market conditions and funding stability; limits the council's ability to adapt if circumstances change","also":["4 other findings in this window","29 papers caught"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-09-23","n_findings":5,"n_documents":29,"top_paper_title":"Capital Programme Update - Appendix 1","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.cornwall.gov.uk/documents/s200484/200484.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":212,"news_drafts_total":1}}